# /etc/waxseal/verify.env — for waxseal-verify@.service
#
#   install -o root -g root -m 0640 verify.env.example /etc/waxseal/verify.env
#
# NO REAL VALUES IN THIS FILE, ever. It is an example committed to a public
# repository. Every value below is a placeholder or a comment.
#
# The unit loads this with a LEADING DASH (EnvironmentFile=-...), so a missing
# file is not a failure — the verifier still runs, against whatever needs no
# credential.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The read credential, if the target needs one.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when the trail is read over HTTP from a chain server. A local
# file path needs none.
#
# A VIEWER or AUDITOR key. NEVER the chain's write key: the variable name is
# shared with the server's bootstrap credential, the value must not be. Mint
# one on the server:
#
#   waxseal-server-admin key-mint --username auditor --label host-verifier
#
#WAXSEAL_API_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The witness credential, if --witness is used below.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A DIFFERENT credential from WAXSEAL_API_KEY, always (REMOTE.md section 8).
# The CLI reads only this variable for witnesses and never hands them the
# chain's key.
#WAXSEAL_WITNESS_API_KEY=

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Extra flags, appended to the ExecStart line.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Word-split by systemd (the unit uses $WAXSEAL_VERIFY_EXTRA_ARGS, not
# ${...}), so several flags in one value work. Paths ABSOLUTE, never `~`.
#
#   --witness URL                 cross-check against a witness. Catches a
#                                 SPLIT VIEW — this server serving you one
#                                 history and someone else another. Only while
#                                 the witness host answers to a DIFFERENT
#                                 authority than the chain server; a witness
#                                 beside the chain it witnesses proves nothing,
#                                 and no code can check which one you named.
#   --expect-anchor-binding       record in the pin state that an anchor
#                                 binding is expected, so a trail that stops
#                                 being anchored becomes visible rather than
#                                 merely quiet.
#   --max-anchor-age-s SECONDS    how stale the newest anchor may be. Unset is
#                                 not the same claim as a very large number:
#                                 unset makes no declaration at all.
#   --tsa-ca-file /path/bundle.pem
#                                 check the RFC 3161 receipts' SIGNATURES, not
#                                 just their structure. Needs the
#                                 waxseal[rfc3161] extra. waxseal names no
#                                 default trust anchor — choosing one would
#                                 decide whom you trust without saying so on
#                                 any line of output. Without this flag the
#                                 receipts are checked structurally only, and
#                                 the run says so out loud.
#
#WAXSEAL_VERIFY_EXTRA_ARGS=--expect-anchor-binding --max-anchor-age-s 90000
